  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 210 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a cutoff shortwave trough just south of\nGrand Junction this afternoon. This trough has plenty of\ninstability associated with it since there are numerous \nthunderstorms across western Colorado and eastern Utah. Meanwhile,\nthere has been plenty of sunshine across our forecast area as the\nhigh cloud cover previously anticipated never fully developed. \nSome weak instability has developed over and near South Park with \nthe SPC mesoanalysis page showing 250 j/kg of surface CAPE and mid\nlevel lapse rates peaking at 8.5 C/km. As the forcing from PVA \nahead of the trough moves closer to Park County, showers and \nperhaps a couple of storms will form late this afternoon. Compared\nto model run yesterday, this system will track a bit further \nsouth. As a result, the vast majority of the precipitation will \nstay south of I-70 with minimal amounts, perhaps a trace to 2\" of \nsnow, forecast near the Eisenhower Tunnel. Conditions still look \nprime for moderate to briefly heavy snow in western and southern \nPark County with forecast amounts between 2-8\" with higher amounts\nin the Mosquito Range. The Winter Weather Advisory is in effect \nfor those areas but it is important to note that there are areas \nin the advisory, the I-70 corridor and northward, that will see \nlittle to no impacts from this system. Some showers may extend \nonto the Palmer Divide but the area of precipitation on the plains\nwill be very minimal. PoPs and QPF were decreased across the \nsouthwestern Denver metro and anything north of I-70. \n\nThere will be brief ridging that moves over Colorado on Thursday\nwhich will lead to dry conditions and seasonal temperatures. \n\nBy Friday, a weak trough with a northerly jet behind it will bring\nsome weak forcing to the mountains of our forecast area. There may\nbe some light snow with amounts staying under 2\" in the mountains\nand no snow below that. \n\nIf you like temperatures in the 60s or low 70s with sunny skies\nand light winds, you will enjoy Saturday through the middle of\nnext week if you live at lower elevations. The bad news is there\nis almost no chance for mountain snowfall Saturday through\nWednesday and that will hurt the already limited snowpack in the\nmountains. It's not until the 3rd week of November when models are\nindicating potential for more widespread precipitation across\nColorado.",
